 note = {Scarlatti's Chefs d’œuvre (1791) was Clementi's first publication of keyboard pieces by his fellow Italian composer. By contributing to the ‘Scarlatti tradition’ in England (Newton, 1939), Clementi followed in the footsteps of other leading eighteenth-century publishers, such as Roseingrave, Johnson, Welcker, and Preston. In this edition he followed the original score closely, despite adding dynamics to appeal to the piano player. In his Introduction to the Art of Playing on the Piano Forte (1801), Clementi printed four Scarlatti pieces—K. 34, 35, 40, and 42—that featured as ‘lessons’ alongside other compositions representing both modern and ‘ancient’ music. This was an integral part of the shift from a scholarly to a pedagogical and performed canon in late-eighteenth-century London (Weber, 1994). Instead of adding dynamics, however, Clementi deviated significantly from Roseingrave’s editions by elongating slurs, joining stems and beams, omitting or specifying ornaments, and, crucially adding fingering for each note, with the aim of teaching the budding pianists of his time the ‘basis of the art of fingering’ (Clementi, 1801). 
Drawing on examples from Clementi’s piano method (1801), I will explore the significance of the ‘small’ form of Scarlatti’s pieces (Sutcliffe, 2009) within Clementi’s fingering system. Rooted in ‘sensationism’ (Helyard, 2022), this system revolves around interconnected hand positions that maintain close contact with the keys. Ultimately, I will demonstrate the crucial role of this system, combined with the aforementioned updates to notation, in the act of reading a musical text on the pianoforte.},
 publisher = {FIMTE},
 title = {Piano ‘Lessons’ by Scarlatti/Clementi: Scarlatti’s Keyboard Pieces in Clementi’s Introduction, Op. 42},
